>
  <h2>The answer depends on where the case is filed</h2>
  <p>
    There is no single statewide rule sending every Florida divorce to
    mediation. Two separate pieces of law decide it, and they do not say the
    same thing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Florida Family Law Rule 12.740</strong> is the permissive one. It
    provides that, except as otherwise provided by law and by the rule, all
    contested family matters and issues <strong>may</strong> be referred to
    mediation, and that every effort must be made to expedite mediation of
    family issues. May, not must.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Section 44.102 of the Florida Statutes</strong> is the mandatory
    one, and it is narrower than people expect. In circuits where a family
    mediation program has been established, once the court finds there is a
    dispute, it <strong>shall</strong> refer all or part of custody,
    visitation, or other parental responsibility issues to mediation.
  </p>
  <p>
    So whether mediation is required turns on two things: whether that circuit
    has established a family mediation program, and whether what you disagree
    about falls within parental responsibility. Past that, individual circuits
    set their own practice through local administrative orders, which is why
    the answer is not the same in Palm Beach as it is in Miami-Dade. The
    <a class="link-underline" href="/areas-served">areas served</a> page
    describes what several South Florida circuits publish about their own
    programs.
  </p>

  <h2>When a court can keep a case out of mediation</h2>
  <p>Two exceptions come up often enough to name.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>A history of domestic violence.</strong> Section 44.102 provides
    that, on the motion or request of a party, a court shall not refer a case
    to mediation if it finds there has been a history of domestic violence
    that would compromise the mediation process. Where domestic violence is a
    factor, the statute also requires the court to offer each party an
    opportunity to consult an attorney or a domestic violence advocate before
    it accepts consent to a referral, and to determine that consent was given
    freely and voluntarily. Among the things the court weighs are acts of
    violence or exploitation, a pattern of behavior exerting power and control
    over the other party, and behavior giving a party reasonable cause to
    believe they are in imminent danger.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Inability to pay.</strong> Court-connected mediation is funded in
    part by a filing fee levied on circuit and county court proceedings. Under
    section 44.108, no mediation fee is assessed against a party the court has
    found to be indigent.
  </p>
  <p>
    Whether either of those applies in a particular case is a finding for the
    court to make, on a request properly put to it. That is a question for an
    attorney who represents you, and not one this page can answer.
  </p>

  <h2>If nothing has been filed yet</h2>
  <p>
    Then none of the above is in play. A referral needs a case, and a case
    needs a filing. Until then there is no judge to refer anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    What is available before filing is private mediation, chosen voluntarily by
    both parties. It involves no order, no referral, and no income threshold.
    Parties often use it to settle terms first, so that what they eventually
    file is uncontested rather than contested.
    <a class="link-underline" href="/process/pre-litigation-disputes">Mediating
    before you file</a> covers that route, and
    <a class="link-underline" href="/process/how-mediation-works">how mediation
    works</a> walks through what a session itself looks like.
  </p>

  <h2>Getting the answer for your own situation</h2>
  <p>
    There are two reliable places to get it. The circuit court where the case
    is or would be filed publishes its local requirements, along with its own
    program's fees and income limits. And an attorney who represents you can
    say what applies to your facts, which is the part that turns on the facts
    rather than on the rule.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the question is how a session is structured, what it costs here, or how
    documents are prepared court-ready, the
    <a class="link-underline" href="/faq">FAQ</a> covers most of it.
  </p>
